A1. These used to cling to the chorus girlies in the good old burlesque days. ~ TIGHTS
A5. These come between the chorus and the baldheaded row. ~ LIGHTS
A9. The way Shakespeare wrote "the." ~ YE
A10. What a one-legged dancer would have to do. ~ HOP
A12. When an actress says this, preceded by the word "I" it usually means a divorce pretty soon. ~ DO
A13. This confronts the girls when they are making up. ~ IMAGE
A14. What Charleston babies have to be. ~ ALERT
A16. These get lit up every night on Broadway. ~ SIGNS
A18. A possible hit of 1930, "Abie's Bulgarian Radish" (init.). ~ ABR
A20. Once more—as, an encore. ~ AGAIN
A22. Slippery fish, not ticket scalpers. ~ EELS
A23. What the heroine called the heartless villain. ~ BRUTE
A24. What the customers have to do to pass out after the show. ~ EXIT
A26. How some shows resembles Volstead. ~ DRY
A28. The comedian rides this in many a Broadway burlesque. ~ HOBBY
A31. What happens to the continuity of the show during the intermission. ~ PAUSE
A34. What do you do after the final curtain. ~ GO
A35. The curse of the shapely chorus cutie. ~ OBESENESS
A36. When you get home if you stop at a night club. ~ AM
A37. This makes the price of tickets higher. ~ TAXES
A39. This man goes high in musical comedies. ~ TENOR
A42. Prancing Queens Association (init).. ~ PQA
A45. How any girl feels about a speaking part. ~ AVID
A48. What George Jean Nathan does for Eugene O'Neil. ~ LAUDS
A49. Half. ~ DEMI
A51. A show that shows. ~ REVUE
A53. The chorus girls lips. ~ RED
A54. The hypotenuse of the triangle play. ~ SIREN
A55. What every ham thinks he deserves. ~ ECLAT
A56. One of the Roman soldiers in Ben-Hur. ~ EXTRA
A57. This expresses likeness or equality ~ AS
A58. How Abie's Irish Rose makes the critics feel. ~ SAD
A60. This expresses nearness or presence. ~ AT
A61. Parlors, bedrooms and baths. ~ SUITES
A62. A method by which the chorus cutie can get anything her little heart desires from the sugardaddy. ~ CRYING

D1. What the stage door Johnnie does for a kiss. ~ TEASES
D2. Songs never sung in revues. ~ HYMNS
D3. Parties no chorus girls desire. ~ TEAS
D4. A chorus cutie's "yes." ~ NO
D6. What most revues lack. ~ IDEA
D7. What hungry chorus girls do at night clubs. ~ GORGE
D8. Sugar Daddy won't last long if he does this with his money. ~ STINTS
D10. You did—if you could hear the words of the opening chorus. ~ HEARD
D11. This is expensive if you give it with champagne. ~ PARTY
D13. An Eskimo opera house. ~ IGLOO
D15. No cutie will walk when these can be procured. ~ TAXIS
D17. That is (abbr.). ~ IE
D19. It is easy to see why this show is popular. ~ BURLESQUE
D21. Fill these two spaces with the only letter in the alphabet of a vaudeville performer. ~ II
D25. A religious society few of the girlies join. ~ ABBEY
D27. What a little kiss did to the pain in her sweetie's heart. ~ EASED
D28. Many a play roasted by the critics does not attain this temperature. ~ HOT
D29. This comes high at the box office. ~ BOX
D30. Chorine's answer to dinner invitations and she doesn't mean maybe. ~ YES
D31. A cutie would love to do this. ~ PET
D32. Broadway is full of sailors when this is in the Hudson (init.). ~ USN
D33. A head ornament. ~ EAR
D38. What you have to be to beat the speculators. ~ ALIVE
D40. A musical offering by a lot of bad actors. ~ OPERA
D41. Comical compositions. ~ FARCES
D42. Actors roles. ~ PARTS
D43. What an extra attraction is. ~ ADDED
D44. Gold-digger's occupation (look out for this one). ~ MINING
D46. Voluptuous Extravaganzas (use just the initials, please). ~ VE
D47. An admission ticket in old Germany. ~ DUCAT
D49. It seems that this kind of play is the only kind the reformers see. ~ DIRTY
D50. The objective of the actor's favorite pronoun. ~ ME
D52. Other besides. ~ ELSE
D54. This is not always as bright as it should be. ~ STAR
D59. An exclamation heard in the front row when the Follies curtain goes up. ~ AH


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A26: How some shows resembles Volstead. → resemble
A48: What George Jean Nathan does for Eugene O'Neil. → O'Neill
A53: The chorus girls lips. → girls'

